# What a derivation is handed and what it may answer (#2186).
#
# An entry that writes `derive conventions/routes` has the compiler call that
# function, during the build, with a `program` describing the modules the
# entry imports. The function answers a list of declarations, and the
# compiler adds them to the entry before it checks anything, so a declaration
# is checked like code someone wrote.
#
# `declare` makes a constant, `name = value`, in the entry. The value is
# data: ints, strings, bools, lists, maps, records and references to
# functions, which is how a route table names its handlers and why the
# checker sees each one. `refuse` makes a compile error at the item `origin`
# names, which is how a convention reports a function it cannot place.
pub type program
  modules

pub type module
  name
  functions
  records

# A pub function as its first arm declares it. An unannotated parameter
# carries "" for its annotation. `reference` is the function itself, so a
# table a derivation declares can hold it and call it.
pub type function
  name
  params
  reference

pub type param
  name
  annotation

pub type record
  name
  fields

pub type constant
  origin
  name
  value

pub type refusal
  origin
  message

pub fn declare origin name value
  constant origin name value

pub fn refuse origin message
  refusal origin message
